<p>
  When we design Plano kitchens, the first material decision is almost always the countertop, and it usually comes down to two choices: quartz and granite. Both look great and perform well, but they are genuinely different materials with different strengths.
</p>

<h2>Quartz: engineered for consistency and ease</h2>
<p>
  Quartz countertops are engineered stone made from crushed natural quartz bound with resin. Because it is manufactured, the pattern is consistent from slab to slab, and it is non-porous — it never needs sealing and resists stains and bacteria.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Best for:</strong> busy family kitchens, light-colored kitchens where consistency matters, and homeowners who want minimal maintenance.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch out for:</strong> manufactured patterns that can look uniform, and some heat sensitivity compared to natural stone — use trivets for hot pans.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Granite: natural character, one of a kind</h2>
<p>
  Granite is a natural stone quarried and cut into slabs. No two slabs are alike, which is exactly why many homeowners love it. It handles heat extremely well and, when sealed properly, resists stains and moisture in everyday use.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Best for:</strong> homeowners who want a one-of-a-kind natural look and don't mind occasional sealing.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch out for:</strong> natural variation (what you pick is what you get), and the need to reseal periodically.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which is more durable?</h2>
<p>
  Both are tough enough for decades of daily kitchen use when installed and cared for properly. Granite is more heat-resistant; quartz is more stain- and maintenance-resistant. For most families, the deciding factor is lifestyle: if you want a surface you can mostly ignore, quartz wins; if you want natural character and don't mind a little routine care, granite is a great pick.
</p>

<h2>Cost considerations</h2>
<p>
  Both quartz and granite sit comfortably within the countertop budget of a typical Plano kitchen remodel, which usually runs <strong>$40,000 to $100,000+</strong> for a full project. Within that range, edge profiles, fabrication, and installation details affect the final number more than the material category itself. Your best move is to look at real slabs in person — the photos online never tell the whole story.
</p>

<h2>Making the choice</h2>
<p>
  There is no universally "right" answer — there is only the right answer for your kitchen. We help Plano homeowners compare real slabs, account for how they cook and entertain, and pick a surface they will still love in ten years.
</p>
